Jahshaka patch 0072 — A LOST DEVICE MUST END THE SESSION, NOT HANG IT.

SOURCE patch (four files, all in the Vulkan render system:
`OgreVulkanRenderSystem.{h,cpp}`, `OgreVulkanDevice.cpp`, `OgreVulkanQueue.cpp`).
EVERY TREE MUST RE-RUN `irisgl/scripts/build-ogre.sh` after pulling it. It shares
no region with any earlier patch.

THE SYMPTOM. Patch 0069 stopped a device loss from ABORTING the process out of
`~VulkanStagingBuffer`. What it uncovered is that the recreate then never
returns: the application freezes with the UI thread inside
`Root::_fireFrameStarted`, for ever. Lane XID-2 caught it with gdb on its own
reproduction (the owner's sequence: hide a plane in an Epic scene), ten minutes
into the hang, the thread at 99.4 % of a core:

    #11 vkDestroyDevice                      libvulkan.so.1
    #12 Ogre::VulkanDevice::destroy          OgreVulkanDevice.cpp:599
    #14 Ogre::VulkanDevice::setPhysicalDevice OgreVulkanDevice.cpp:914
    #15 Ogre::VulkanRenderSystem::handleDeviceLost   OgreVulkanRenderSystem.cpp:1505
    #16 Ogre::VulkanRenderSystem::validateDevice     OgreVulkanRenderSystem.cpp:1462
    #17 Ogre::Root::_fireFrameStarted                OgreRoot.cpp:928
    (below #11: a busy loop on clock_gettime inside libnvidia-glcore.so.595.84)

So the block is `vkDestroyDevice` ITSELF, not a wait we could bound: on this
driver, destroying a VkDevice whose channel is in `CTX SWITCH TIMEOUT` spins
until the channel is reclaimed, which after an Xid 109 does not happen.

AND THE RECREATE COULD NOT RECOVER ANYWAY. `handleDeviceLost` rebuilds the pin's
own resources, but a host that holds Vulkan objects on that VkDevice outside the
render system (Jahshaka's ray-query tier: pipelines, descriptor pools, BLAS/TLAS
memory) is never told, and the first command it records afterwards names a dead
device. A working recreate is a program, not a patch.

THE FIX, in three parts:

 1. `validateDevice` no longer calls `handleDeviceLost()` for a LOST device. It
    logs one critical line naming the kernel log, and returns false. A device
    ELECTION on a live device (`forceDeviceElection` picking a different GPU)
    still recreates, because that path destroys a healthy device and does not
    block. Root treats the false as a veto on the frame, so the application does
    not render again -- it is expected to notice (`RenderSystem::isDeviceLost()`
    is public) and end with a message. Jahshaka's engine does exactly that.
 2. `VulkanDevice::destroy` skips `vkDeviceWaitIdle` when the device is lost. The
    spec says that call returns VK_ERROR_DEVICE_LOST; this driver may block, and
    everything submitted is finished by definition once the device is gone.
 3. `VulkanQueue::destroy` skips the same call for the same reason.

WHAT THIS PATCH DOES NOT DO: it does not make `vkDestroyDevice` safe. A host that
wants to survive the report must end the process without an orderly Vulkan
teardown (Jahshaka's does: it logs and `_exit`s). That is the honest shape until
somebody owns a real recreate.

VERIFICATION. `git apply --check` on a tree carrying 0001-0071 and
`git apply --reverse --check` after applying. On the XID-2 reproducer with patch
0071 REMOVED (so the device is still lost on purpose): the app no longer hangs --
it logs "THE DEVICE WAS LOST AND IS NOT RECREATED", the engine's own fatal line
follows, and the process ends; measured against the same reproducer's hangs
before it. `--engine-selftest` unchanged (no pixel path is touched); the engine
suites green.

diff --git a/RenderSystems/Vulkan/src/OgreVulkanRenderSystem.cpp b/RenderSystems/Vulkan/src/OgreVulkanRenderSystem.cpp
--- a/RenderSystems/Vulkan/src/OgreVulkanRenderSystem.cpp
+++ b/RenderSystems/Vulkan/src/OgreVulkanRenderSystem.cpp
@@ -1456,8 +1456,36 @@
             }
         }
 
-        // recreate logical device with all resources
-        if( anotherIsElected || mDevice->isDeviceLost() )
+        // A LOST DEVICE IS NOT RECREATED (Jahshaka patch 0072). handleDeviceLost()
+        // ends in VulkanDevice::setPhysicalDevice -> destroy() -> vkDestroyDevice,
+        // and on NVIDIA 595.84 after an Xid 109 CTX SWITCH TIMEOUT that call does
+        // not return: it spins at 100 % of a core inside libnvidia-glcore for
+        // ever (lane XID-2 caught it with gdb on the owner's own reproduction --
+        // the frame below is Root::_fireFrameStarted, so the UI thread never comes
+        // back and the application is frozen, not crashed). A hang is worse than
+        // an end for a user.
+        //
+        // It would not be a recovery either: the host keeps Vulkan objects of its
+        // own on this VkDevice (Jahshaka's ray-query tier is one), and nothing
+        // tells them a new device exists. So this render system answers the only
+        // question it can answer honestly -- the device is gone -- and leaves the
+        // ending to the host, which is expected to say so and quit.
+        if( mDevice->isDeviceLost() )
+        {
+            if( !mReportedDeviceLost )
+            {
+                mReportedDeviceLost = true;
+                LogManager::getSingleton().logMessage(
+                    "Vulkan: THE DEVICE WAS LOST AND IS NOT RECREATED. Check the system log for "
+                    "an NVRM Xid / GPU reset around this time. The application must end.",
+                    LML_CRITICAL );
+            }
+            return false;
+        }
+
+        // recreate logical device with all resources (a device ELECTION on a live
+        // device, not a loss)
+        if( anotherIsElected )
         {
             handleDeviceLost();
 
diff --git a/RenderSystems/Vulkan/src/OgreVulkanDevice.cpp b/RenderSystems/Vulkan/src/OgreVulkanDevice.cpp
--- a/RenderSystems/Vulkan/src/OgreVulkanDevice.cpp
+++ b/RenderSystems/Vulkan/src/OgreVulkanDevice.cpp
@@ -580,7 +580,13 @@
     {
         if( mDevice )
         {
-            vkDeviceWaitIdle( mDevice );  // intentionally ignore result in destroy()
+            // NOT ON A LOST DEVICE (Jahshaka patch 0072). The spec says this
+            // returns VK_ERROR_DEVICE_LOST; this driver may instead block until a
+            // hung channel is reclaimed, which after an Xid 109 is not bounded.
+            // Everything submitted is finished by definition once the device is
+            // lost, so there is nothing to wait for.
+            if( !isDeviceLost() )
+                vkDeviceWaitIdle( mDevice );  // intentionally ignore result in destroy()
 
             mGraphicsQueue.destroy();
             destroyQueues( mComputeQueues );
